OS-9/68K FREEWARE COLLECTION  --  start here===========================================Community software for OS-9/68000, with whatever documentation and sourcecould be found for each program and a record of where it came from.  Someof it has source here; much of it has none that survived.Most programs need no runtime module at all.  The rest are starred inDOC/INDEX because they use Microware's cio, and cio, csl, csl020, math andmath881 are on this disk by Microware's permission, so nothing has to befetched.TO TRY SOMETHING: DOC/START-HERE is a dozen programs that run with nothingset up -- no arguments, no data to find, no terminal type to get right --and says how to get back out of each.TO FIND SOMETHING: DOC/CATEGORIES groups every program by what it is for --shells, editors, games, archivers -- which is the order to read in beforeyou know the names.  DOC/INDEX is the alphabetical list and says more abouteach one.  Both are generated from the disk itself.OS-9 itself is Microware's, and still a current product.  This is the softwarethe community wrote for it.------------------------------------------------------------------STARTING IT------------------------------------------------------------------TWO WAYS.  The first needs nothing but this disk.1.  STANDALONE -- no Microware modules at all        OS9DISK=/absolute/path/to/this/disk  os9exec /dd/CMDS/bash /dd/SYS/login    SYS/login sets PATH, HOME, TERM and TERMCAP and hands you an    interactive shell.  Started bare instead, bash has none of them, so    no command is found and the full-screen programs look locked up when    they are only missing TERM.  Setting HOME is also what makes bash    read /dd/.bashrc, where cd and pwd are defined -- bash's own pwd    hangs the shell here.  See DOC/README-RUNNING.    bash is a public-domain build and is this disk's shell.  Verified    running with this disk as the ONLY disk and no shell, cio, csl or math    module present anywhere.    RUN COMMANDS BY THEIR FULL PATH:        /dd/CMDS/cookie        /dd/CMDS/soundex </dd/SYS/motd        /dd/CMDS/GAMES/advent            (after: cd /dd/GAMES/ADV)    bash's PATH search does not work against this filesystem -- setting PATH    and typing a bare `cookie' gives "command not found" even though the    program is there and runs perfectly when named in full.  Absolute paths    always work.  This is a wart, not a broken disk.    Do NOT write OS9DISK=./disk with a leading "./" -- modules still load but    every ordinary file open fails.  Use an absolute path.2.  WITH YOUR OWN OS-9 SYSTEM        OS9DISK=/path/to/this/disk  OS9H1=/path/to/your/os9/system        os9exec -r /h1/CMDS/shell    This disk is /dd, so every program finds its own data; Microware's shell,    its utilities, and cio/csl/math come from /h1.  Bare command names work,    and so do the starred programs, whose modules ship here.    Everything on this disk was tested this way.------------------------------------------------------------------AS A SINGLE DISK IMAGE------------------------------------------------------------------    This collection also ships as one RBF disk image, with room left free    for score files, saves and your own work.        OS9DISK=/path/to/dd  os9exec /dd/CMDS/bash    The image carries proper OS-9 owner and permission bits, which a host    directory cannot: every module is e+pe+r+pr, every data file r+pr with    no execute.  On a host directory those bits are whatever your Mac or    Linux box happened to have, which is meaningless here.    The image file must be named `dd' (or h0..hz as a secondary device):    os9exec resolves a disk image by FILENAME, so `freeware.rbf' will not    mount.  That is an os9exec limitation, not a property of the image.------------------------------------------------------------------WHERE TO LOOK------------------------------------------------------------------    DOC/STATUS        what runs, what needs cio, what does not run.  START HERE    DOC/INDEX         one line saying what every program IS    DOC/ORIGINS       one line saying where every program CAME FROM    DOC/README-DOCS   the five ways to find out how to use something    DOC/README-RUNNING  deployment, mtools, DOS disks, GNU chess    DOC/EFFO-INFO     the authors' own descriptions AND licence conditions    DOC/README-FLOATINGPOINT   math.l is single precision; math881.l is not    DOC/README-BUSERR   the five programs that take a bus error, and why    DOC/README-MODULES  the files in CMDS that are not commands    SOURCES.txt       provenance, build method, and what was left out and why    DOC/<program>/    per-program documentation    SRC/<package>/    source, where it survived------------------------------------------------------------------WHAT IS ON IT------------------------------------------------------------------    CMDS/, CMDS/GAMES/   the commands and the games                   (including cio, math, math881, csl and csl020 --                    Microware's runtime modules, here by permission)    CMDS/BROKEN/    programs that do not run, kept because they deserve                    attention    CMDS/REBUILT/   alternates, other editions and other-CPU builds    CMDS/GCC139/, CMDS/GCC2/          GNU C 1.39 and 2.x    SBPROLOG/      SB-Prolog 2.2 with its whole module library    SRC/, DOC/     source where it survived, and documentation    DOC/CATEGORIES lists every program grouped by what it is for, with a    count for each group.  It is generated from the disk, so it cannot go    stale; a list typed out here would, and had.------------------------------------------------------------------LICENCE------------------------------------------------------------------    Public domain and freeware throughout.  FOUR programs carry a condition    their authors asked for -- "no military use" -- and one of those is by    os9exec's own author.  They are named in DOC/EFFO-INFO and marked in    DOC/INDEX.  Please pass the condition on with the software.    The only Microware files here are the five runtime modules named above,    included with their permission; SOURCES.txt records the exchange.  An    earlier CMDS/NONFREE directory is gone: of the six binaries in it, four    turned out not to be Microware's at all and are back in CMDS, and two    were theirs and were removed.